issue_context.body:
We should come to an agreement on the following properties:

1, `label` should not be required, it's being phased out of Zephyr overall

2. `sensor-index` should not be specified by the application, it's assigned by the subsystem and should not be guaranteed to be the same across builds. Having to specify this manually makes it difficult to add/remove new entries in the middle.

3. `vendor`, `model`, and `friendly-name` should not be required at the individual node level, it's vulnerable to having a mismatch. Since there will be one node per sensor type here, several nodes may point to a single physical sensor.

4. I'm not sure why `minimal-interval` needs to be specified here. It's a component of the physical driver and can be attained through the sensor API.

5. `rotation-matrix` like `vendor` etc should not be here either, a physical sensor may have multiple of these nodes (1 per type) and this is likely to cause errors.

PROPOSAL

1. Remove label, there's no use for it

2. Remove `sensor-index` it should be assigned by the subsystem using `DT_` macros

3. Create a new binding for sensor metadata with all fields marked as optional. Many applications may not even have a UI and reporting the vendor/model/friendly-name is useless to them. The only required field is the sensor phandle and it should map to an existing HW sensor which we're describing.
```
properties:
  vendor:
    type: string
    required: false
  model:
    type: string
    required: false
  friendly-name:
    type: string
    required: false
  rotation-matrix:
    type: array
    required: false
  sensor:
    type: phandle
    required: true
```

4. Remove minimal interval, it's also not needed.

*UPDATE*
With the assumption that #49294 will merge, the scope of this issue is reduced as `vendor`, `model`, `friendly-name`, and `rotation-matrix` are handled.

The issue to discuss is what to do with `sensor-index` and `minimal-interval`. With the following reasoning:
1. `sensor-index` in device tree can be hard to manage because of overlays. Setting a base level DTS with sensor index 0, 1, 2 followed by adding another base DTS for another board with 0, 1. Then an overlay ontop of these might want to set index 3? This becomes impossible to manage. I believe we should add some kind of mapping for a default one (index 0) but nothing else. This would look like:

```
sensor_subsystem {
  compatible = "zephyr,sensor-subsystem";
  sensor-type-defaults {
    /* Can use a compatible or hard coded relative path to the parent node (I would prefer the path)
     * I've considered putting this as a direct array so it would look like:
     *     sensor-type-defaults = <SENSOR_TYPE_ACCEL &lid_accel
     *                             SENSOR_TYPE_GYRO &base_gyro>;
     * While this reads nicer than having child nodes, it makes it hard to override just 1 in an overlay.
     */
    accel {
      sensor-type = "SENSOR_TYPE_ACCEL";
      handle = <&lid_accel>;
    };
    gyro {
      sensor-type = "SENSOR_TYPE_GYRO";
      handle = <&base_gyro>;
    };
  };
  lid_accel: lid_accel {
    /* Normal instantiation of the sensor subsystem accessor */
    provider = <&lid_imu>;
  };
  lid_gyro: lid_gyro {
    /* Normal instantiation of the sensor subsystem accessor */
    provider = <&lid_imu>;
  };
  base_accel: base_accel {
    /* Normal instantiation of the sensor subsystem accessor */
    provider = <&base_imu>;
  };
  base_gyro: base_gyro {
    /* Normal instantiation of the sensor subsystem accessor */
    provider = <&base_imu>;
  };
};
```

2. Having `minimal-interval` is redundant, this value should be gotten from the provider's driver. If the provider changes, we don't want to have to keep hunting these values down and having to update them.

comments:
> Can you give an example dts snippet of use here. Vendor, model info should effectively be encoded in the compatible so those don't make sense to me. Not sure about friendly-name either. At least from a standard zephyr binding point of view.

Sorry @galak, I didn't get a notification on this comment. Here's a sample of what I have in mind (it's crude and not 100% correct syntax), but keep in mind that I created this issue to discuss those specific properties listed in the description

```
/ {
  sensor_subsystem {
    compatible = "zephyr,sensor_subsystem";
    lid_sensor_orientation {
      compatible = "zephyr,sensor_subsystem_rotation";
      matrix = <-1 0 0
                 0 1 0
                 0 0 1>;
      apply-to = <&bmi160>;
    };
    lid_accel {
      compatible = "zephyr,sensor_subsystem_imu";
      physical_sensor = <&bmi160>;
      sensor_type = SENSOR_SUBSYSTEM_SENSOR_TYPE_ACCEL;
    };
    lid_gyro {
      compatible = "zephyr,sensor_subsystem_imu";
      physical_sensor = <&bmi160>;
      sensor_type = SENSOR_SUBSYSTEM_SENSOR_TYPE_GYRO;
    };
    base_accel {
      compatible = "zephyr,sensor_subsystem_imu";
      physical_sensor = <&bma255>;
      sensor_type = SENSOR_SUBSYSTEM_SENSOR_TYPE_ACCEL;
    };
    lid_angle {
      compatible = "zephyr,sensor_subsystem_angle";
      accelerometers  = <&lid_accel &base_accel>;
    };
};
```

Note that in this example we allocate 2 slots for sensor data that both direct requests to the same physical sensor. The virtual lid angle sensor then references these slots instead of the physical sensor. The reason is that otherwise you won't be able to arbitrate the sample rates needed for the physical sensors. They need to be wrapped once only per sensor type. This also leverages features in the Sensor V2 API #44098

index does not need to persistent across builds, @MaureenHelm suggested the use of chosen nodes:

```
chosen {
  zephyr,sensor-subsystem-default-accel = &lid_accel;
};
```

[chosen nodes](https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/build/dts/intro.html?highlight=chosen#aliases-and-chosen-nodes)

`minimal-interval` will be queried at runtime during initialization and possibly during a refresh if power state changes.
